# AI Safety Rules

## No silent write actions

- The app (and any AI/agent code within it) must never perform a write action
  against an external system (Jira, Confluence, GitHub, Discord, Gmail, git
  push/commit, Figma edits, etc.) without an explicit, in-the-moment user
  confirmation for that specific action.
- "Explicit confirmation" means a real user click/approval tied to the
  specific action about to be taken — not a one-time global setting, not an
  assumed consent from earlier conversation.
- Read operations (fetching, listing, summarizing) do not require
  confirmation. Only writes/mutations do.

## Evidence over assertion

- AI-generated tasks must never assert a claim without linking to the
  evidence that produced it. If the LLM router returns a task/insight with no
  traceable source, discard it or route it to Unclear rather than displaying
  it as fact.
- Do not let the LLM fabricate next actions or done criteria that aren't
  grounded in the underlying evidence.

## Ambiguity handling

- When the LLM is uncertain about task ownership or the requirement itself,
  it must not force a confident answer. Surface it in the Unclear bucket
  instead of hallucinating an owner or scope.

## Central router enforcement

- Any code path that reaches an LLM provider must go through the central LLM
  router (see `architecture.mdc`). This is also a safety boundary: it's where
  prompt injection mitigation, output validation, and audit logging of AI
  actions live. Do not bypass it for "quick" or "internal" calls.
